WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.230
Ever since "Game of Thrones" became a major
hit for HBO, networks have been searching

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.740
for the next big epic fantasy series to mimic
that kind of success.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:14.070
Netflix found its own success with "The Witcher,"
a show that broke viewership records at the

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.430
time of its first season's release.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:21.099
Amazon, on the other hand, has doubled down
on its hopes for fantasy-laden success with

00:00:21.099 --> 00:00:25.910
both a series based on "The Wheel of Time"
books and a show tackling the Second Age of

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:27.480
Tolkien's Middle-earth.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.890
It's an ambitious and expensive move for the
company, but one that shows Amazon's dedication

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.280
to the fantasy genre and its fans.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.500
"Whatever happens now, there's no turning
back."

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.680
"The Wheel of Time" is based on the book series
of the same name by authors Robert Jordan

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.840
and Brandon Sanderson, which spans 14 main
novels and a handful of other works set in

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:52.010
the universe, including — perhaps most relevantly
— a prequel novella entitled "New Spring."

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:57.550
Jordan died in 2007, two years after the 11th
book had been released, which left the future

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.450
of the series in doubt at the time.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:04.500
However, epic fantasy up-and-comer Sanderson
took over and, with detailed notes from Jordan

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.170
and oversight by Jordan's wife and longtime
editor, completed the final three books and

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.860
brought the series to a satisfying close.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.140
Since then, fans have been left wondering
how long it would take for an adaptation to

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.140
come to fruition.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.460
Well, thanks to Amazon, that wait is over.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.310
Here's what we know so far about the upcoming
first season of "The Wheel of Time" show.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.860
According to the official synopsis,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:30.310
"The story follows Moiraine, a member of the
shadowy and influential all-female organization

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:35.369
called the 'Aes Sedai' as she embarks on a
dangerous, world-spanning journey with five

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:37.240
young men and women.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:42.090
Moiraine believes one of them might be the
reincarnation of an incredibly powerful individual,

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:46.340
whom prophecies say will either save humanity
or destroy it."

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:49.660
This description falls more or less in line
with the plot of the first book, "The Eye

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:53.700
of the World," though judging by the first
trailer, we're going to be seeing quite a

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.200
bit of action that happens off-page in the
books, and even meeting a few characters that

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.110
don't factor in until later.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:05.250
Unlike the binge model that Netflix uses,
Amazon is continuing its trend of weekly episode

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.250
releases.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:10.361
However, binge watchers need not fret entirely,
as the first three episodes of "The Wheel

00:02:10.361 --> 00:02:14.720
of Time" will drop all at once on Amazon Prime
Video on November 19.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.250
From that point forward, episodes will be
released weekly all the way through December

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:19.330
24.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.110
There will be eight episodes of Season 1 in
total.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.970
While those who enjoy binge watching may be
disappointed by the news that "The Wheel of

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:30.470
Time" will release episodes on a weekly basis
as opposed to all at once, this is certainly

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.750
a strategy that's worked well in the past.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.920
One could argue that talking and theorizing
was half the fun of watching shows like "Game

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.110
of Thrones" and "Lost" in real time with everybody
else.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:44.000
The production of Season 1 was hampered by
COVID-related delays, but the wait shouldn't

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.300
be so long for Season 2.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.660
Amazon's betting big on the show's performance,
and has already greenlit the sophomore set.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.200
Production commenced last summer.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.340
Given that "The Wheel of Time" books are high
fantasy, they contain quite a large assortment

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:57.340
of characters.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:02.010
It's even become a joke among the fandom that
keeping track of named characters is a challenging

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.010
task.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:05.950
Amazon's adaptation will have to make some
adjustments in this regard, but many of the

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:10.760
core cast members are already in place, with
one very notable exception.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.790
Arguably the biggest cast member will be Rosamund
Pike.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:18.130
She will portray Moiraine Damodred, an Aes
Sedai of the Blue Ajah.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:23.070
The Aes Sedai are a faction of women who wield
the One Power, this universe's version of

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.070
magic.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:28.430
Josha Stradowski will play Rand al'Thor, who
serves as the major protagonist of the book

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:29.430
series.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:34.650
Marcus Rutherford and Barney Harris will step
into the roles of Perrin Aybara and Mat Cauthon,

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:35.650
respectively.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:39.731
It's important to note that the role of Cauthon
has been recast for Season 2, so don't fall

00:03:39.731 --> 00:03:42.950
too deeply in love with Harris' portrayal
in Season 1.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:47.940
Some of the other cast members for "The Wheel
of Time" Season 1 include Zoë Robins as Nynaeve

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:53.220
al'Meara, Madeleine Madden as Egwene al'Vere,
the girl covered in paint in the trailer;

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:59.000
and Daniel Henney as Lan Mandragoran, Moiraine's
Warder and the heir to a lost throne.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.570
The one glaring omission?

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.600
Where in Randland is Elayne?

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.910
The casting of the Daughter-Heir of Andor
has yet to be revealed, as of the making of

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:11.450
this video, despite her playing a crucial
role in "The Eye of the World."

